      "LineEndingsOnly": true,
      "BeforeSourceContent": "# Erlang.Net\r\n\r\nAn experimental C#/.NET implementation targeting Erlang/OTP **29.1.1**, pinned to `ad05823719d77c8faee87348ea39513d4e2f99c5`. The full assignment is not complete. See [progress](docs/PROGRESS.md), [compatibility](docs/COMPATIBILITY.md), [detailed changelog](CHANGELOG.md) and [next steps](docs/NEXT_STEPS.md).\r\n\r\nDevelopment validation now compares **778 expressions**, **4027 comparable separately compiled fixture modules** and **132 compiler-diagnostic fixtures** against the pinned reference. The module track compiles generated C# Release assemblies and reference BEAM modules; selected outcomes match, full optimizer/debug/resource compatibility remains unfinished. [Verified evidence](docs/validation/part-214/strict-guard.json).\r\n\r\nTwo map iterator fixtures explicitly use `-compile(no_ssa_opt)` because the pinned OTP optimizer assumes a map-only argument for `maps:map/2`. The initial optimized differences and one corrected provisional record-guard expectation remain documented in [the original comparison](docs/validation/part-102/differential.json) and [the source audit](docs/validation/part-104/reference-audit.json).\r\n\r\nThe implemented subset runs on .NET 10 without BEAM. It includes immutable terms, selective receive, local processes/links/monitors, an Erlang parser and evaluator, generated C# modules, selected Erlang gen_server/supervisor callback adapters and a mixed C#/Erlang MSBuild preprocessor.\r\n\r\nAll 91 lists exports are registered and directly tested; [lists compatibility scope](docs/LISTS_COMPATIBILITY.md) records functional proof and runtime boundaries.\r\n\r\nAll 34 maps exports are registered and directly tested; [maps compatibility scope](docs/MAPS_COMPATIBILITY.md) describes functional proof and remaining runtime boundaries.\r\n\r\nSelected Erlang behaviour adapters expose ten gen_server and fourteen supervisor entry points. See [adapter usage and limitations](docs/OTP_ADAPTERS.md).\r\n\r\n[Module readiness](docs/MODULE_READINESS.md) reports delivered/remaining component milestones, tested registered MFAs and changes from the previous checkpoint. Its API-presence percentages measure declarations, not complete compatibility. The full inventory is in [module-readiness.json](docs/module-readiness.json).\r\n\r\nRegenerate the report from the current successful test report and actual runtime registry:\r\n\r\n```powershell\r\ndotnet run --project tools/Erlang.Tool --no-build -- readiness docs artifacts/tests.json docs\r\n```\r\n\r\nTerm.A(\"ok\") represents the Erlang atom ok, a symbolic value often used as a success marker; Term.String(\"ok\") creates a character list. The factory reuses ten fixed common immutable atom values and leaves arbitrary Atom objects uncached; their names are retained in the VM-wide name table for existing-atom conversion. Compare terms by value, not CLR object identity. Domain constants retain their separate protocol meanings.\r\n\r\nThe source subset includes map construction, associative/exact updates and map patterns with supported guard-expression keys, plus integer/binary bitstring construction and patterns with signed extraction, byte order and prior-segment size bindings. Overall project completion is estimated at roughly 10% after the verified early functional platform milestone; this is an engineering estimate, not verified compatibility coverage. See progress and the changelog for evidence and remaining scope.\r\n\r\n```powershell\r\ndotnet build\r\ndotnet run --project tests/Erlang.Tests --no-build -- artifacts/tests.json\r\ndotnet run --project examples/HelloHybrid --no-build\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe example prints `Hello World`, receives a stop message, and checks a compiled `.erl` module returning 42. Native Erlang syntax appears in an ordinary `.cs` file:\r\n\r\n```csharp\r\nvar result = receive\r\n    {hello, Name} when is_list(Name) ->\r\n        io:format(\"Hello ~s~n\", [Name]), ok;\r\n    {stop} -> stop\r\nend.\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe enclosing method/lambda must be async and supply `ProcessContext erlangProcess`. Erlang constructs currently begin at C# statement/assignment/return/lambda-body boundaries; `end.` terminates each block. Blocks are independent Erlang scopes. C# locals are not implicitly imported into them. Synchronous suspension, arbitrary nested hybrid expressions and IDE language services are pending.\r\n\r\nSDK 10.0.400 is pinned in `global.json`. On restricted hosts that prevent MSBuild worker processes, use `dotnet build -m:1`.\r\n\r\nCompile Erlang source to C#:\r\n\r\n```powershell\r\ndotnet tools/Erlang.Tool/bin/Debug/net10.0/Erlang.Tool.dll compile examples/HelloHybrid/arithmetic.erl artifacts/arithmetic.g.cs\r\n```\r\n\r\nGenerate the pinned source inventory without changing the reference checkout:\r\n\r\n```powershell\r\ndotnet tools/Erlang.Tool/bin/Debug/net10.0/Erlang.Tool.dll inventory ../otp docs\r\n```\r\n\r\nValidate build integration and a local NuGet consumer:\r\n\r\n```powershell\r\npwsh -File tools/validate.ps1\r\n```\r\n\r\nNo packages are published. The prototype package targets net10.0. Install the local tool package to run `dotnet erlang compile INPUT OUTPUT`; without installation, use the DLL command above. Compilation currently emits AST construction code evaluated by the C# runtime, rather than direct native lowering of every operation.\r\n\r\nSource formatting: `dotnet run --project tools/Erlang.Style -- --write .`. The full validation script checks layout with `--check`. This development tool uses the pinned SDK's Roslyn formatter and repository-specific spacing/argument rules; it does not process native-Erlang hybrid Program.cs. Detailed numbered work history is indexed in [CHANGELOG](CHANGELOG.md); append only to its latest volume, rolling over at 32 KiB or 400 lines.\r\n\r\nTests are a dependency-free executable harness and return a failing exit code on any failed assertion. `dotnet test` is not the test entry point. A separate differential runner requires an exact OTP 29.1.1 development oracle; production execution and builds do not.\r\n\r\nThe erlang module currently registers **287/351** pinned exports; [scope ledger](docs/ERLANG_COMPATIBILITY.md) lists64 missing APIs. Real external-process ports include the13 port APIs, line/packet IO, owned monitors/aliases/priority/exit/link lifecycle, output and signal queue pressure, and actual in/out, shared stderr, argv0, auxiliary stdio and Windows overlapped options across170 source scenarios. [Part214](docs/validation/part-214/strict-guard.json) verifies4937/4937 original comparisons and5544/full same-head CI. The437 registered MFAs are directly tested;native drivers/fd, other executable formats/platforms, complete metadata/errno/GUI/resource/scheduling/distribution/upstream/performance contracts remain unfinished. API presence is not semantic completion.\r\n\r\nCurrent fd increment: borrowed Linux descriptors/Windows handles have real pipe/file IO,packet/line/EOF and cancellation checks. Part215 original run completed4946/4951 with five new fd metadata/settings mismatches;the separate inherited fd track passed3/8. Part216 corrects os_pid to {os_pid,undefined} and rejects exit_status;5568/full Windows local checks pass,patched original proof is pending. See [repair and retained evidence](docs/changelog/0031.md). TTY/control/flag and broader resource/platform compatibility remain partial;API presence remains erlang287/351.\r\n\r\nCorrected fd verification: [Part217](docs/validation/part-217/strict-guard.json) passes4951/4951 corpus plus8/8 inherited fd IO and5568/full exact-head Linux CI. Earlier initial failures are preserved in216;TTY/flags/ABI/resources/platform and whole erlang compatibility remain partial.\r\n\r\nSelected spawn_opt verification: [Part219](docs/validation/part-219/strict-guard.json) passes4997/4997 corpus plus8/8 inherited fd IO and5618/full exact-head Linux CI. The additional fd IO remains passing;heap/GC/CPU priority/connected distribution and whole erlang compatibility remain partial.\r\n",
